  "abstract": "<p>\n                    We introduce the volume function for\n                    <inline-formula content-type=\"math/mathml\">\n                      <mml:math xmlns:mml=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper C Superscript normal infinity\">\n                        <mml:semantics>\n                          <mml:msup>\n                            <mml:mi>C</mml:mi>\n                            <mml:mrow class=\"MJX-TeXAtom-ORD\">\n                              <mml:mi mathvariant=\"normal\">\n                                ∞\n                                \n                              </mml:mi>\n                            </mml:mrow>\n                          </mml:msup>\n                          <mml:annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C^{\\infty }</mml:annotation>\n                        </mml:semantics>\n                      </mml:math>\n                    </inline-formula>\n                    -hermitian invertible sheaves on an arithmetic variety as an analogue of the geometric volume function. The main result of this paper is the continuity of the arithmetic volume function. As a consequence, we have the arithmetic Hilbert-Samuel formula for a nef\n                    <inline-formula content-type=\"math/mathml\">\n                      <mml:math xmlns:mml=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper C Superscript normal infinity\">\n                        <mml:semantics>\n                          <mml:msup>\n                            <mml:mi>C</mml:mi>\n                            <mml:mrow class=\"MJX-TeXAtom-ORD\">\n                              <mml:mi mathvariant=\"normal\">\n                                ∞\n                                \n                              </mml:mi>\n                            </mml:mrow>\n                          </mml:msup>\n                          <mml:annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C^{\\infty }</mml:annotation>\n                        </mml:semantics>\n                      </mml:math>\n                    </inline-formula>\n                    -hermitian invertible sheaf. We also give other applications, for example, a generalized Hodge index theorem, an arithmetic Bogomolov-Gieseker’s inequality, etc.\n                  </p>",
